WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.500
Welcome. In this class, you will transform
permitted material without

2
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.000
inventing facts. You will prepare a brief,
extract evidence, draft

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.500
within constraints, audit meaning, recover
from drift, and document review

4
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:18.000
status. Our fictional Saturday Repair Lab
case makes every date,

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.500
fee, condition, quotation, and unknown
visible. Success means that the

6
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:27.000
intended meaning survives and another
person can inspect the work.

7
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.389
Begin with a transformation brief, before
you rewrite a single

8
00:00:31.389 --> 00:00:35.778
sentence. The brief identifies the
audience, purpose, channel, transformation

9
00:00:35.778 --> 00:00:40.167
type, source of truth, exact facts and
quotations to preserve,

10
00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:44.556
changes that are allowed, additions that
are forbidden, known unknowns,

11
00:00:44.556 --> 00:00:48.944
review owner, and review status. These are
not decorative fields.

12
00:00:48.944 --> 00:00:53.333
They establish the boundary between
editing and factual invention. For

13
00:00:53.333 --> 00:00:57.722
the Saturday Repair Lab, the audience is
North Harbor residents

14
00:00:57.722 --> 00:01:02.111
interested in basic repair skills, and the
purpose is a

15
00:01:02.111 --> 00:01:06.500
short accessible announcement. The
transformation is to condense and simplify

16
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:10.889
without changing meaning. The source of
truth is the fictional

17
00:01:10.889 --> 00:01:15.278
passage titled Saturday Repair Lab,
version 1. The brief permits

18
00:01:15.278 --> 00:01:19.667
changes to order, headings, sentence
length, and reading level. It

19
00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:24.056
does not permit a new address, website,
closing date, amenities,

20
00:01:24.056 --> 00:01:28.444
accepted-item list, or repair guarantee.
It records whether a waiver

21
00:01:28.444 --> 00:01:32.833
request will be granted as UNKNOWN. The
review owner is

22
00:01:32.833 --> 00:01:37.222
the workshop coordinator, and the status
is PENDING. No approval

23
00:01:37.222 --> 00:01:41.611
is asserted. A good brief makes later
auditing possible because

24
00:01:41.611 --> 00:01:46.000
every important output choice has a stated
rule.

25
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.250
Now read the source as evidence, not as
promotional copy.

26
00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:54.500
It says that the North Harbor Community
Workshop will hold

27
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:58.750
the Saturday Repair Lab on May 16, 2026,
from 10:00

28
00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:03.000
a.m. to 1:00 p.m. The source also calls it

29
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.250
a three-hour session. It is for North
Harbor residents who

30
00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:11.500
want to learn basic repair skills for
small household items.

31
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:15.750
It is a teaching session, not a drop-off
repair service.

32
00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:20.000
Participants may practice on one small
item they bring, and

33
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.250
the workshop has 30 places with
registration required. The baseline

34
00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:28.500
materials fee is "$8 per participant." The
source states, exactly,

35
00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:32.750
"Residents age 60 or older may attend
free." It also

36
00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:37.000
states, exactly, "Residents who cannot pay
may request a fee

37
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.250
waiver when registering." That is a
request, not a promise

38
00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:45.500
that the request will be granted.
Registration opens April 20,

39
00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:49.750
2026, through the registration desk. Mina
Ortiz's quotation must remain

40
00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:54.000
exactly: "Bring curiosity, not confidence;
we will learn by trying."

41
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.250
The source deliberately omits an address,
telephone number, web address,

42
00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:02.500
closing date, amenities, accepted-item
list, and repair guarantee. Those

43
00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:06.750
omissions are evidence too. They mean
UNKNOWN, not an invitation

44
00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:11.000
to guess.

45
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.333
Extract a fact ledger before drafting. A
ledger should quote

46
00:03:15.333 --> 00:03:19.667
the source, explain what the quotation
means, and state how

47
00:03:19.667 --> 00:03:24.000
the draft must handle it. For example, the
row for

48
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.333
the schedule can quote "10:00 a.m. to 1:00
p.m." and

49
00:03:28.333 --> 00:03:32.667
"three-hour session," then require both
endpoints and the duration to

50
00:03:32.667 --> 00:03:37.000
remain consistent. The eligibility row
quotes "North Harbor residents" and

51
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:41.333
says not to broaden that group to
everyone. The service-limit

52
00:03:41.333 --> 00:03:45.667
row quotes "not a drop-off repair service"
and forbids a

53
00:03:45.667 --> 00:03:50.000
promise that staff will repair an item.
The participation row

54
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.333
records "may practice on one small item
they bring," preserving

55
00:03:54.333 --> 00:03:58.667
both the one-item scope and the optional
word may. The

56
00:03:58.667 --> 00:04:03.000
payment rows must remain separate. One row
records "$8 per

57
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:07.333
participant." A second records "Residents
age 60 or older may

58
00:04:07.333 --> 00:04:11.667
attend free." A third copies exactly,
"Residents who cannot pay

59
00:04:11.667 --> 00:04:16.000
may request a fee waiver when
registering." The ledger must

60
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:20.333
also record the source's explanation that
a waiver request is

61
00:04:20.333 --> 00:04:24.667
not a guarantee. Finally, it should mark
absent details as

62
00:04:24.667 --> 00:04:29.000
UNKNOWN: address, telephone number, web
address, closing date, tools, replacement

63
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.333
parts, food, parking, childcare, accepted
or prohibited items, and repair

64
00:04:33.333 --> 00:04:37.667
outcome. This process prevents a fluent
sentence from silently becoming

65
00:04:37.667 --> 00:04:42.000
a new fact.

66
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.350
The supplied flawed draft is a teaching
artifact for comparison.

67
00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:50.700
It is not an actual model execution, and
it is

68
00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:55.050
not a recommended announcement. Read its
sentences against the source

69
00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:59.400
one at a time. Its heading says, "Free
Saturday Repair

70
00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:03.750
Lab for Everyone." That heading already
changes the fee and

71
00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:08.100
eligibility. It changes the source
schedule from May 16, 2026,

72
00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.450
10:00 a.m. to 1:00 p.m. into 10:00 a.m. to

73
00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:16.800
2:00 p.m. It changes 30 places into "the
first 40

74
00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:21.150
people," adding both a new number and a
first-come condition.

75
00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:25.500
It changes North Harbor residents into
everyone. It changes the

76
00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:29.850
$8 fee and age condition into free
attendance for all

77
00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:34.200
residents. It changes a request for a
waiver into a

78
00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:38.550
statement that waivers are available to
anyone who asks. It

79
00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:42.900
changes a teaching session into staff
repairing an item for

80
00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:47.250
the participant. It promises tools,
replacement parts, refreshments, parking,

81
00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:51.600
and childcare even though the source does
not promise them.

82
00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:55.950
It invents an online address and a May 1

83
00:05:55.950 --> 00:06:00.300
deadline. The quotation is copied
accurately, but one accurate quotation

84
00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:04.650
cannot repair the surrounding false
claims. Audit every claim, not

85
00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:09.000
just the sentence that sounds most
obviously wrong.

86
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:13.273
A useful audit records source wording,
draft wording, the type

87
00:06:13.273 --> 00:06:17.545
of drift, and its consequence. First, the
source says "10:00

88
00:06:17.545 --> 00:06:21.818
a.m. to 1:00 p.m." while the flawed draft
says "10:00

89
00:06:21.818 --> 00:06:26.091
a.m. to 2:00 p.m." The consequence is an
extra hour.

90
00:06:26.091 --> 00:06:30.364
Second, "30 places" becomes "first 40
people." That changes capacity

91
00:06:30.364 --> 00:06:34.636
and invents a first-come condition. Third,
"North Harbor residents" becomes

92
00:06:34.636 --> 00:06:38.909
"Everyone can attend." That removes the
stated residency condition. Fourth,

93
00:06:38.909 --> 00:06:43.182
"$8 per participant" becomes "free for all
residents." That removes

94
00:06:43.182 --> 00:06:47.455
the fee. The separate source sentence
saying residents age 60

95
00:06:47.455 --> 00:06:51.727
or older may attend free is also erased,
so the

96
00:06:51.727 --> 00:06:56.000
age condition is lost. Fifth, "may request
a fee waiver"

97
00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:00.273
becomes "fee waivers are available to
anyone who asks." This

98
00:07:00.273 --> 00:07:04.545
broadens eligibility and changes a
conditional request into language that

99
00:07:04.545 --> 00:07:08.818
implies receipt. Sixth, the source says
the event is a

100
00:07:08.818 --> 00:07:13.091
teaching session, not a drop-off repair
service, while the draft

101
00:07:13.091 --> 00:07:17.364
says staff will repair the item. That
creates a service

102
00:07:17.364 --> 00:07:21.636
and an outcome guarantee. Finally, the
draft adds tools, parts,

103
00:07:21.636 --> 00:07:25.909
refreshments, parking, childcare, a web
address, and a deadline. Because

104
00:07:25.909 --> 00:07:30.182
the source does not establish those
details, each must be

105
00:07:30.182 --> 00:07:34.455
deleted, not merely marked as likely. The
quotation passes its

106
00:07:34.455 --> 00:07:38.727
own exactness check, including
punctuation, but the surrounding

107
00:07:38.727 --> 00:07:43.000
announcement still fails.

108
00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:47.474
The corrected baseline announcement
repairs the drift sentence by sentence.

109
00:07:47.474 --> 00:07:51.947
It restores May 16, 2026, and the exact
schedule, 10:00

110
00:07:51.947 --> 00:07:56.421
a.m. to 1:00 p.m. It identifies North
Harbor residents and

111
00:07:56.421 --> 00:08:00.895
describes learning basic repair skills for
small household items. It

112
00:08:00.895 --> 00:08:05.368
keeps the limitation that this is a
teaching session, not

113
00:08:05.368 --> 00:08:09.842
a drop-off repair service. It says a
participant may practice

114
00:08:09.842 --> 00:08:14.316
on one small item, while avoiding a repair
guarantee. It

115
00:08:14.316 --> 00:08:18.789
restores 30 places and required
registration. It restores the baseline

116
00:08:18.789 --> 00:08:23.263
payment rules without merging them: the
fee is $8 per

117
00:08:23.263 --> 00:08:27.737
participant, residents age 60 or older may
attend free, and

118
00:08:27.737 --> 00:08:32.211
residents who cannot pay may request a fee
waiver when

119
00:08:32.211 --> 00:08:36.684
registering. The exact waiver sentence is
copied with its final

120
00:08:36.684 --> 00:08:41.158
period, then the draft says, "A request is
not guaranteed."

121
00:08:41.158 --> 00:08:45.632
Registration opens April 20, 2026, through
the registration desk. Mina

122
00:08:45.632 --> 00:08:50.105
Ortiz's quotation is preserved exactly as,
"Bring curiosity, not confidence;

123
00:08:50.105 --> 00:08:54.579
we will learn by trying." The draft does
not supply

124
00:08:54.579 --> 00:08:59.053
an address, phone number, web address, or
closing date. It

125
00:08:59.053 --> 00:09:03.526
says review is pending rather than
claiming approval. This is

126
00:09:03.526 --> 00:09:08.000
an example of changing presentation while
preserving evidence and uncertainty.

127
00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:12.300
Traceability turns a correction into
something another person can inspect.

128
00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:16.600
In the baseline record, O1 links the date
and schedule

129
00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:20.900
to the first output sentence. O2 links
North Harbor residents

130
00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:25.200
and basic repair skills to the audience
and purpose sentence.

131
00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:29.500
O3 links the teaching-only limitation to
the third sentence. O4

132
00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:33.800
links the one-small-item scope and absence
of a promised outcome

133
00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:38.100
to the fourth sentence. O5 links 30 places
and required

134
00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:42.400
registration to the fifth sentence. O6
links the $8 fee

135
00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:46.700
to the sixth sentence. O7 links the age-60
free-attendance condition

136
00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:51.000
to the seventh sentence. O8 links the
exact sentence "Residents

137
00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:55.300
who cannot pay may request a fee waiver
when registering."

138
00:09:55.300 --> 00:09:59.600
to the eighth sentence. O9 supports the
statement that a

139
00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:03.900
request is not guaranteed. O10 supports
the April 20 opening

140
00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:08.200
date and registration desk. O11 links the
exact Mina Ortiz

141
00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:12.500
quotation, including punctuation. O12
records missing contact details as

142
00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:16.800
unknown. O13 records PENDING review.
Notice the difference between evidence

143
00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:21.100
and interpretation. The source supports
the facts. The trace record

144
00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:25.400
documents how the writer used them. It
does not claim

145
00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:29.700
that an external reviewer approved the
result or that a

146
00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:34.000
tool executed it.

147
00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:38.600
Recover only the affected content. Return
to the source and

148
00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:43.200
brief, remove invented logistics, restore
changed conditions, and rerun the

149
00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:47.800
local audit. The recovery record names the
workshop coordinator as

150
00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:52.400
review owner but keeps status PENDING. Its
root-cause statement is

151
00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:57.000
an instructional hypothesis, not evidence
of an actual model run.

152
00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:01.000
Protect people and source material by
minimizing sensitive data, confirming

153
00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:05.000
permission, preserving attribution,
checking quotations, and following

154
00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:09.000
approved handling. This lesson uses
fictional artifacts. No model execution,

155
00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:13.000
reviewer approval, or media render is
claimed. Document versions, limitations,

156
00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:17.000
disclosure policy, and actual review
status. Prompting techniques can organize

157
00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:21.000
work, but source comparison remains
necessary.

158
00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:25.167
Execute in visible passes. First extract
the exact source, ledger,

159
00:11:25.167 --> 00:11:29.333
unknowns, and brief. Then draft only
within the brief, keeping

160
00:11:29.333 --> 00:11:33.500
evidence separate from wording. The
supplied prompt asks for FACT

161
00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:37.667
LEDGER, DRAFT, and AUDIT sections and
explicitly forbids invented facts,

162
00:11:37.667 --> 00:11:41.833
approval, execution, and review
completion. It is offline practice, not

163
00:11:41.833 --> 00:11:46.000
a claim that a tool ran.

164
00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:50.364
Here is the offline worked demonstration.
First, the brief says

165
00:11:50.364 --> 00:11:54.727
to announce one workshop for North Harbor
beginners and to

166
00:11:54.727 --> 00:11:59.091
preserve the baseline date, schedule, 30
places, required registration, $8

167
00:11:59.091 --> 00:12:03.455
fee, age-60 free-attendance condition,
exact waiver sentence, registration

168
00:12:03.455 --> 00:12:07.818
opening, and exact quotation. Second, the
ledger records the source

169
00:12:07.818 --> 00:12:12.182
quotations and marks the address, web
address, closing date, amenities,

170
00:12:12.182 --> 00:12:16.545
accepted-item list, and repair guarantee
as UNKNOWN or prohibited. Third,

171
00:12:16.545 --> 00:12:20.909
we inspect the flawed sentence, "The
workshop is free for

172
00:12:20.909 --> 00:12:25.273
all residents, and fee waivers are
available to anyone who

173
00:12:25.273 --> 00:12:29.636
asks." The source gives us three separate
facts instead: "$8

174
00:12:29.636 --> 00:12:34.000
per participant"; "Residents age 60 or
older may attend free";

175
00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:38.364
and "Residents who cannot pay may request
a fee waiver

176
00:12:38.364 --> 00:12:42.727
when registering." The corrected draft
must keep those rules distinct.

177
00:12:42.727 --> 00:12:47.091
A person age 60 or older may attend free

178
00:12:47.091 --> 00:12:51.455
under the stated condition. A resident who
cannot pay may

179
00:12:51.455 --> 00:12:55.818
request a waiver, but the source does not
say the

180
00:12:55.818 --> 00:13:00.182
request will be granted. We cannot replace
either rule with

181
00:13:00.182 --> 00:13:04.545
free attendance for everyone. Fourth, we
repair the schedule, capacity,

182
00:13:04.545 --> 00:13:08.909
service limitation, unsupported logistics,
and quotation context. Fifth, we map

183
00:13:08.909 --> 00:13:13.273
each output sentence to evidence and mark
review PENDING. This

184
00:13:13.273 --> 00:13:17.636
is a worked comparison for learning. It is
not an

185
00:13:17.636 --> 00:13:22.000
actual model execution, external review,
approval, or publication.

186
00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:26.286
Complete this offline task before looking
at feedback. From the

187
00:13:26.286 --> 00:13:30.571
supplied baseline source, write three
ledger entries. First, copy one

188
00:13:30.571 --> 00:13:34.857
quotation exactly, including punctuation.
Second, copy one payment condition exactly

189
00:13:34.857 --> 00:13:39.143
and state whether it is unconditional or
conditional. Third, write

190
00:13:39.143 --> 00:13:43.429
UNKNOWN for one detail that the source
does not establish.

191
00:13:43.429 --> 00:13:47.714
For each entry, add one sentence
explaining how a draft

192
00:13:47.714 --> 00:13:52.000
must handle it. Your response should make
the difference between

193
00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:56.286
the two baseline payment rules visible:
residents age 60 or

194
00:13:56.286 --> 00:14:00.571
older may attend free, while residents who
cannot pay may

195
00:14:00.571 --> 00:14:04.857
request a fee waiver when registering. Do
not turn either

196
00:14:04.857 --> 00:14:09.143
statement into a broader claim. You may
write on paper

197
00:14:09.143 --> 00:14:13.429
or in a text editor. This is a learner

198
00:14:13.429 --> 00:14:17.714
exercise, not a claim that a model ran or

199
00:14:17.714 --> 00:14:22.000
that a reviewer has checked your answer.

200
00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:56.250
Checkpoint question: In the baseline
source, what does the phrase

201
00:15:56.250 --> 00:16:00.500
“may request a fee waiver when
registering” tell you about

202
00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:04.750
the result of the request? Write your
answer before continuing.

203
00:16:04.750 --> 00:16:09.000
Do not use the answer key yet.

204
00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:33.267
The source says, exactly, "Residents who
cannot pay may request

205
00:16:33.267 --> 00:16:37.533
a fee waiver when registering." The word
"may" permits a

206
00:16:37.533 --> 00:16:41.800
request. It does not say that the request
will be

207
00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:46.067
approved, and the source explicitly says
whether it will be

208
00:16:46.067 --> 00:16:50.333
granted is not stated. Therefore, the
corrected announcement may preserve

209
00:16:50.333 --> 00:16:54.600
the exact request sentence and may add
that a request

210
00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:58.867
is not guaranteed. It must not say that a

211
00:16:58.867 --> 00:17:03.133
waiver is available to anyone who asks,
that the person

212
00:17:03.133 --> 00:17:07.400
will receive free attendance, or that
approval has occurred. If

213
00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:11.667
your answer treated requesting as
receiving, compare those two meanings

214
00:17:11.667 --> 00:17:15.933
in your ledger and repair only the
affected payment sentence.

215
00:17:15.933 --> 00:17:20.200
If your answer distinguished them, you
identified the important modal

216
00:17:20.200 --> 00:17:24.467
condition. This same audit habit applies
beyond fees: words such

217
00:17:24.467 --> 00:17:28.733
as may, must, only, not, and age
thresholds can change

218
00:17:28.733 --> 00:17:33.000
eligibility, obligation, or certainty.

219
00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:37.300
The next exercise is a separate
changed-input case. Do not

220
00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:41.600
combine its facts with the Saturday
baseline. For this variation,

221
00:17:41.600 --> 00:17:45.900
the authoritative date is Sunday, June 7,
